WEBVTT

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.820
Help all kids learn and grow with PBS Kids. Thank you for supporting your PBS

00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:14.200
station. We're here in North America, in the western part of the Great Plains, the

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:21.600
land of the short grass prairie. It's us, the Kraft Brothers. Not there. Over here. This way.

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:30.000
I'm Chris. I'm Martin. And we're hanging with a herd of bison. When you're

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:33.480
around a herd of bison you have to make sure you don't startle them or make them

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:37.620
nervous because that's when they could stampede and you don't want to get

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:42.360
trampled by a herd of stampeding bison when you check out the bison long enough

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:46.680
you notice that there's one animal who doesn't seem to worry about being close

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:51.760
to the bison in fact he wants to get as close to the bison as he possibly can

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:58.040
I'm talking about the buffalo bird wherever the bison go the buffalo birds

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:00.000
The bison are roaming.

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:03.440
So the buffalo birds have to be roamers, too.

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:09.420
They hang out right near the bison's mouth and hooves, just waiting for any insect to

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:13.460
scurry out from the mouth of the bison that they can then pick off.

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:18.790
They've really got to be on their toes, because they can easily get munched by a big bison

00:01:18.790 --> 00:01:21.930
mouth or stepped on and smooshed by their hooves.

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:24.340
Okay, the black ones are the males.

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:30.000
The brown ones are the females, but they both love the tasty insects that the buffaloes

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:36.400
It's a great example of one animal using the help of another animal to get something it needs.

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:37.940
In this case, food.

00:01:37.940 --> 00:01:42.040
What a cool little creature with a really strange behavior.

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:46.460
And there are more unexpected creature behaviors out here on the prairie.

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:53.110
Imagine if we could check out some of the strangest, most bizarre, most mysterious creature powers on the prairie.

00:01:53.110 --> 00:01:54.770
What if...

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:43.050
Then lizard climb, falcon flight, and land ride.

00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:54.320
Mystery on the Prairie.

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:57.310
We're going to have to move again.

00:02:57.310 --> 00:02:59.440
How much time do we have till they get here?

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:12.230
two minutes two minutes prepare for liftoff Jimmy my bus bike huh these bison

00:03:12.230 --> 00:03:17.490
never stop moving they're like an army of hairy tanks just rolling across the

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:24.660
prairie since I've been tracking them the herd roams about three miles every day and every creature

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:30.000
out here knows you gotta clear out when bison come through you don't want to be caught in the middle

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:31.640
of a crowd of bison.

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:35.490
All right, BuzzBike's safe and in her cradle.

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:40.120
- Jimmy, liftoff, pronto!

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:41.960
- He's not here, remember?

00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:43.580
He's out collecting buffalo berries

00:03:43.580 --> 00:03:46.500
for his famous buffalo berry pie.

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:47.400
- Uh-oh.

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:50.400
- Who's gonna drive the Tortuga?

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:54.390
- Oh, let's get out here.

00:03:54.390 --> 00:03:55.360
- Jimmy!

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:56.630
- Easy, Jay-Z.

00:03:56.630 --> 00:03:59.050
Those bison have you really nervous, huh?

00:03:59.050 --> 00:04:00.000
- Bison?

00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:04.760
I'm talking about the buffalo bush. You wouldn't believe what I saw

00:04:05.250 --> 00:04:07.940
freaky like a mystery freaky

00:04:08.210 --> 00:04:13.760
Freaky? Mystery? Oh, this I gotta hear. Was it a creature or what?

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:18.210
Sorry to interrupt your little chit-chat, but we gotta go now

00:04:19.340 --> 00:04:21.340
Come on, Jimmy to the cockpit

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:24.020
We gotta see this

00:04:24.020 --> 00:04:26.370
Let's get out there quick

00:04:26.370 --> 00:04:30.000
It's too late

00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:32.740
Yup, we're surrounded.

00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:37.670
Ooh, lifting off now would cause a stampede, and we can't do that.

00:04:37.670 --> 00:04:39.650
We're stuck!

00:04:39.650 --> 00:04:41.700
Gotta wait till they pass through, guys!

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:42.720
Chris?

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:43.670
Martin?

00:04:43.670 --> 00:04:45.650
Hey!

00:04:45.650 --> 00:04:49.000
Where'd they go?

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:55.900
Whoa!

00:04:55.900 --> 00:04:56.900
Jimmy was right!

00:04:56.900 --> 00:04:58.960
This is weird.

00:04:58.960 --> 00:04:59.980
Who did that?

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:04.980
that? Hung all those insects up like that? I don't know, but we've got another

00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:10.090
creature mystery on our hands, bro. A weasel? Gotta be some kind of predator.

00:05:10.090 --> 00:05:17.830
Only one way to find out. A stakeout! We'll get mini-sized, so whoever it is

00:05:17.830 --> 00:05:29.730
won't even see us spying on them. Get your eyes! Alright.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:33.140
The mystery of the prairie thornbush has begun.

00:05:33.140 --> 00:05:34.890
Woo-hoo!

00:05:40.180 --> 00:05:44.840
Okay, all is quiet right now.

00:05:44.840 --> 00:05:55.350
Just a cute little gray birdie at the top of that bush.

00:05:55.350 --> 00:05:57.530
Ooh, check it out!

00:05:57.530 --> 00:06:00.000
Prairie king snake!

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:01.760
basking down below.

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:03.200
Huh?

00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:07.910
He got the snake!

00:06:07.910 --> 00:06:10.020
What?

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:16.090
That little songbird thinks he's an eagle or something.

00:06:16.090 --> 00:06:17.510
I know.

00:06:17.510 --> 00:06:20.110
He just has little stick legs, not talons.

00:06:20.110 --> 00:06:21.310
And he's tiny.

00:06:21.310 --> 00:06:22.740
Who is that guy?

00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:24.610
Whoa!

00:06:24.610 --> 00:06:29.580
It's another one!

00:06:29.580 --> 00:06:30.000
Ah!

00:06:42.830 --> 00:06:45.290
He's taking Martin clear across the prairie.

00:06:45.290 --> 00:06:47.590
Help!

00:06:47.590 --> 00:06:52.470
What is with these little gray birds?

00:06:52.470 --> 00:06:55.840
You again, with a snake.

00:06:55.840 --> 00:06:57.130
I don't believe it.

00:06:57.130 --> 00:06:58.240
It's him.

00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:00.000
That's the guy who's been thorning things.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:04.550
The Little Masked Gray Songbird! Mystery solved!

00:07:04.550 --> 00:07:06.440
Uh-oh.

00:07:06.440 --> 00:07:10.610
Martin, those gray songbirds, they're the ones doing the thorning!

00:07:10.610 --> 00:07:14.560
What?

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:17.200
Uh, you're not going to...

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:21.090
Uh, Chris? I've been thorned!

00:07:21.090 --> 00:07:26.630
Look, I don't know why they're doing this, but I've almost got an idea on these birds.

00:07:30.250 --> 00:07:33.970
Aha! It's a loggerhead Shrike!

00:07:33.970 --> 00:07:38.930
Wait a second, that female Shrike is checking out all the things he caught.

00:07:38.930 --> 00:07:44.170
It just proved to her that he's good at catching things, and that means-

00:07:44.170 --> 00:07:48.440
Ahem! Chris! Did you really think we wouldn't find you?

00:07:48.440 --> 00:07:50.270
What are you doing at that thorn bush?

00:07:50.270 --> 00:07:53.560
Well, we've almost solved Jimmy's mystery and-

00:07:53.560 --> 00:07:56.380
Uh oh, that's what I was afraid of.

00:07:56.380 --> 00:07:57.560
What?

00:07:57.560 --> 00:07:58.740
Look!

00:08:00.190 --> 00:08:01.430
They're eating!

00:08:01.430 --> 00:08:04.390
These little birds are surprisingly tough predators,

00:08:04.390 --> 00:08:06.210
but they don't have big, strong talon feet

00:08:06.210 --> 00:08:07.790
like hawks and eagles do.

00:08:07.790 --> 00:08:10.110
So they use thorn bushes.

00:08:10.110 --> 00:08:13.090
The thorns hold their prey while they peck at it.

00:08:13.090 --> 00:08:15.820
That's why they hang their prey up like that.

00:08:15.820 --> 00:08:18.020
- So he's the guy that did that?

00:08:18.020 --> 00:08:19.480
Weird.

00:08:19.480 --> 00:08:21.380
- And, oh no, Martin.

00:08:21.380 --> 00:08:23.370
Martin!

00:08:23.370 --> 00:08:24.860
- Where, where, what?

00:08:24.860 --> 00:08:26.270
- Yo, Chris.

00:08:26.270 --> 00:08:27.650
You gotta get out of there.

00:08:27.650 --> 00:08:30.000
Those strikes hang up their prey just before they eat.

00:08:30.220 --> 00:08:31.120
Eat it!

00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:32.140
What?!

00:08:37.800 --> 00:08:41.590
Uh, listen, uh, Spike.

00:08:41.590 --> 00:08:44.480
Uh, can I call you that? Spike the Shrike?

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:47.310
Uh, you might not know this, but I don't taste very good.

00:08:48.760 --> 00:08:50.870
Uh-oh! Guys, quick!

00:08:50.870 --> 00:08:55.040
You gotta get out here before he comes back and decides to eat me!

00:08:55.040 --> 00:08:58.000
Don't worry, Martin. I've got your coordinates. We're coming!

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:13.280
I wish we could just teleport him back here!

00:09:13.280 --> 00:09:14.360
Me too!

00:09:14.360 --> 00:09:18.010
But the teleporter doesn't work on living creatures.

00:09:18.010 --> 00:09:18.920
Never has!

00:09:18.920 --> 00:09:21.040
Chris, you go!

00:09:21.040 --> 00:09:23.150
Already on my way!

00:09:25.640 --> 00:09:30.000
Yikes! I'm blown away!

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:31.000
Block two?

00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:32.530
Somebody!

00:09:32.530 --> 00:09:33.530
Hurry!

00:09:33.530 --> 00:09:47.500
Can you see me?

00:09:47.500 --> 00:09:49.800
Oh yeah.

00:09:49.800 --> 00:09:52.660
This buffalo bush disguise is really working.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:03.200
Nothing but a buffalo bush here.

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:24.840
My cheek, thorn bush, definitely not one of my better disguises.

00:10:35.920 --> 00:10:39.510
Hi! Uh, have you seen a mass gray bird around here?

00:10:39.510 --> 00:10:45.200
You see, I was wondering if you could help me get off this thorn before he gets back.

00:10:45.200 --> 00:10:50.320
Haha! Yeah!

00:10:50.320 --> 00:10:51.480
I'm Dethor!

00:10:51.480 --> 00:10:53.850
Uh-oh!

00:10:53.850 --> 00:10:56.060
Whoa!

00:10:56.060 --> 00:10:56.860
Uh!

00:10:56.860 --> 00:10:57.810
Whoa!

00:10:57.810 --> 00:10:58.360
Oh!

00:10:58.360 --> 00:11:00.000
Haha!

00:11:00.380 --> 00:11:04.420
That's what I call a lucky kratfall, right into a bird's nest.

00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:10.760
Great, Martin. Jimmy will teleport you the Butterfly or XT, and you can fly out of there.

00:11:11.630 --> 00:11:15.970
Ah, no problem. I'll just chill out with these eggs until it gets here.

00:11:16.250 --> 00:11:19.260
Pretty eggs. I wonder whose nest this is.

00:11:19.900 --> 00:11:24.730
Okay, special delivery to Martino. And...

00:11:24.730 --> 00:11:30.000
Great! Look at this. This egg looks totally different than the other.

00:11:30.700 --> 00:11:31.700
This shouldn't happen.

00:11:31.700 --> 00:11:35.940
I mean, the eggs in a bird nest should all look pretty much the same.

00:11:35.940 --> 00:11:40.300
So why is this egg different than all the rest?

00:11:40.300 --> 00:11:45.200
Chris, we've got a new mystery on our hands.

00:11:45.200 --> 00:11:46.200
We do?

00:11:46.200 --> 00:11:50.660
I mean, we do?

00:11:50.660 --> 00:11:51.690
Yeah.

00:11:51.690 --> 00:11:54.570
The mystery of the scrambled eggs.

00:11:54.570 --> 00:11:57.940
There's an egg in this nest that doesn't belong here.

00:11:57.940 --> 00:11:58.940
That is weird.

00:11:58.940 --> 00:12:00.000
But what did Dave do?

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:01.360
for creature mysteries.

00:12:01.360 --> 00:12:03.420
Sit tight, bro, I'm on my way.

00:12:03.420 --> 00:12:05.060
- Wait a second, guys.

00:12:05.060 --> 00:12:07.260
We don't even know whose nest that is.

00:12:07.260 --> 00:12:08.700
And this may be our last chance

00:12:08.700 --> 00:12:10.390
to pull Martin out of there.

00:12:10.390 --> 00:12:13.410
- Yeah, but we never abandon a creature mystery.

00:12:13.410 --> 00:12:14.870
No way, no how.

00:12:15.800 --> 00:12:18.260
Yeah, and look, a feather.

00:12:18.260 --> 00:12:20.820
It's from the bird whose nest this is.

00:12:20.820 --> 00:12:23.180
If you can make me a creature power disc from it,

00:12:23.180 --> 00:12:25.760
I'll fit right in and have the power of flight

00:12:25.760 --> 00:12:28.050
if I need to get out of here.

00:12:28.050 --> 00:12:29.470
- Reverse teleport.

00:12:30.440 --> 00:12:32.930
Got it.

00:12:32.930 --> 00:12:35.450
We better hurry.

00:12:35.450 --> 00:12:39.270
Who knows what that bird will do if she comes back and finds a mini crab bro in her nest.

00:12:39.270 --> 00:12:47.810
It says here that shrikes are the only predatory songbirds,

00:12:47.810 --> 00:12:50.850
and they sometimes catch things even bigger than themselves,

00:12:50.850 --> 00:12:55.850
like mice, lizards, voles, frogs, and even other birds.

00:12:55.850 --> 00:12:57.800
What? How?

00:12:57.800 --> 00:12:59.700
They're just cute little birdies.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:05.350
Well, one thing they have is a really tough, strong beak with a notch in it.

00:13:05.350 --> 00:13:10.010
While most raptors use strong-clawed feet to catch their prey, the Shrike's main weapon

00:13:10.010 --> 00:13:11.010
is that beak.

00:13:11.010 --> 00:13:15.930
Other than that, and their thorning behavior, they're just little songbirds through and

00:13:15.930 --> 00:13:16.820
through.

00:13:16.820 --> 00:13:19.710
I should be able to have this suit worked out in a jiff.

00:13:19.710 --> 00:13:20.660
Ew!

00:13:20.660 --> 00:13:22.160
Jimmy, are these your snacks?

00:13:22.160 --> 00:13:23.160
Yep.

00:13:23.160 --> 00:13:25.800
If it's good enough for a Shrike, it's good enough for me.

00:13:25.800 --> 00:13:26.790
Yum!

00:13:26.790 --> 00:13:30.000
Buffalo berry pie.

00:13:31.630 --> 00:13:35.390
The only thing I'm worried about is, can you remember where you put all your snacks?

00:13:35.390 --> 00:13:40.240
A Shrike has a great memory, remembering where all the food stashes are for months.

00:13:40.240 --> 00:13:41.460
No problem.

00:13:41.460 --> 00:13:45.390
I never forget where I put a piece of pepperoni pizza.

00:13:45.390 --> 00:13:50.100
Loggerhead Shrike disc complete.

00:13:50.100 --> 00:13:53.200
Coming at you, Jimmy!

00:13:53.200 --> 00:13:56.850
And south!

00:13:56.850 --> 00:13:58.350
The disc!

00:13:58.350 --> 00:13:59.760
Ah, just in time.

00:14:03.070 --> 00:14:05.960
Because here comes Mama Shrike!

00:14:05.960 --> 00:14:13.130
Activate longer hand Shrike power!

00:14:13.130 --> 00:14:17.380
Uh-oh, I turned into a Shrike egg instead of an adult Shrike.

00:14:17.380 --> 00:14:22.060
Oh, of course, my other hand was touching the egg, so that's what I turned into.

00:14:22.060 --> 00:14:24.410
Hey, it's dark inside here.

00:14:24.410 --> 00:14:29.540
It kind of cramped, but at least it's warm.

00:14:32.490 --> 00:14:35.560
I gotta get to that nest!

00:14:35.560 --> 00:14:38.500
I can't believe I'm missing this mystery.

00:14:38.500 --> 00:14:41.630
This bison herd never ends.

00:14:41.630 --> 00:14:43.630
Buffalo berry bush coming through.

00:14:43.630 --> 00:14:49.820
Ooh, and now a bunch of buffalo birds too?

00:14:49.820 --> 00:14:52.540
Hey, excuse me, guys.

00:14:52.540 --> 00:14:55.710
Wow, the males are much shinier than the females.

00:14:55.710 --> 00:14:58.710
Hey, why are you guys always following the bison around anyway?

00:14:58.710 --> 00:15:00.000
Aren't you worried about getting

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:01.940
getting trampled or chomped?

00:15:01.940 --> 00:15:03.500
Remember the prairie rule.

00:15:03.500 --> 00:15:08.370
Everybody get out of the way when the bison come through.

00:15:08.370 --> 00:15:11.530
The grasshoppers know the rule.

00:15:11.530 --> 00:15:13.970
Hey, you caught one.

00:15:13.970 --> 00:15:16.640
Amazing, you do know that rule.

00:15:16.640 --> 00:15:19.220
And that's exactly why you hang around them.

00:15:19.220 --> 00:15:21.620
To catch the insects that clear out of the way

00:15:21.620 --> 00:15:23.400
when the bison roam through.

00:15:23.400 --> 00:15:26.900
Another bird, another clever hunting style.

00:15:26.900 --> 00:15:28.870
No wonder they're called buffalo birds,

00:15:28.870 --> 00:15:30.000
or sometimes cowbirds.

00:15:30.320 --> 00:15:33.180
because they go everywhere the cow like bison do.

00:15:46.230 --> 00:15:48.330
It's getting pretty toasty in here.

00:15:48.330 --> 00:15:51.960
Anybody ready to come out yet?

00:15:51.960 --> 00:15:54.860
Yes, it's time.

00:15:54.860 --> 00:15:58.360
Got her, the shell.

00:16:00.120 --> 00:16:17.170
Happy birthday! Everybody made it! Except the mystery chick. Come on, little dude, you can do it!

00:16:24.450 --> 00:16:30.000
Yes! Now, who are you? I'm running a match to our database of prairie birds.

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:36.710
It's a buffalo bird, also known as Cowbird!

00:16:36.710 --> 00:16:37.650
A cowbird?

00:16:37.650 --> 00:16:40.920
Well, that'll call you Moo!

00:16:40.920 --> 00:16:43.900
And you're Thorn, and how 'bout Spear?

00:16:43.900 --> 00:16:45.880
And over there, Spike Jr.

00:16:45.880 --> 00:16:46.880
Is that okay, Mom?

00:16:46.880 --> 00:16:50.770
She must be getting some food.

00:16:50.770 --> 00:16:53.770
But Martin, how did a buffalo bird end up in a shrike nest?

00:16:53.770 --> 00:16:56.690
Yeah, where are Moo's parents?

00:16:56.690 --> 00:16:59.690
Yeah, we gotta find Moo's mom and dad.

00:16:59.690 --> 00:17:00.000
We've got to find Moo's mom and dad.

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:01.600
Gotta get 'em home.

00:17:01.600 --> 00:17:03.020
Let's call Chris.

00:17:03.020 --> 00:17:05.870
Maybe he can find Moo's mom.

00:17:05.870 --> 00:17:12.250
Honored Aviva, I'll ask around.

00:17:12.250 --> 00:17:16.780
Hey guys, anybody lose their chick?

00:17:16.780 --> 00:17:22.880
Excuse me, do you know anyone looking for their chick?

00:17:22.880 --> 00:17:25.860
This is gonna be tougher than I thought.

00:17:30.480 --> 00:17:33.130
The herd has passed through. We're good to go.

00:17:33.130 --> 00:17:38.490
Alright, so let's get in there and airlift Martin and the lost chick out of that nest.

00:17:38.490 --> 00:17:42.500
And by the time we do that, hopefully Kryzla found Moo's parents.

00:17:42.500 --> 00:17:46.540
Jimmy, is this yours?

00:17:46.540 --> 00:17:48.760
Sure is!

00:17:48.760 --> 00:17:49.400
Ow!

00:17:49.400 --> 00:17:52.920
What are my snack stashes? Shrike style.

00:17:52.920 --> 00:17:53.910
*sigh*

00:17:54.840 --> 00:17:56.420
Good luck!

00:17:56.420 --> 00:17:57.360
Thanks!

00:18:04.670 --> 00:18:07.330
Whoa! It's feeding time!

00:18:07.330 --> 00:18:09.450
And it's every chick for himself!

00:18:10.770 --> 00:18:14.020
It takes two busy parents to feed all these hungry mouths!

00:18:14.630 --> 00:18:18.180
Hey! Moo got some too!

00:18:18.180 --> 00:18:21.540
Isn't that nice of them feeding a chick that's not even theirs?

00:18:21.540 --> 00:18:25.160
Thanks! That'll buy us a little more time to find his parents.

00:18:35.210 --> 00:18:39.340
Gotta keep him thinking I'm a chick, or they may feed me to the birds.

00:18:39.340 --> 00:18:43.370
Martin, we've got a clear shot to the buffalo bush.

00:18:43.370 --> 00:18:48.850
So we can just dart in, drop a rescue line, and fly out of there so fast Spike and Mama

00:18:48.850 --> 00:18:50.230
Shrike won't even notice.

00:18:50.230 --> 00:18:52.100
You'll have to be fast.

00:18:52.100 --> 00:18:56.070
In and out in less than four minutes, because that's how often these hardworking parents

00:18:56.070 --> 00:18:58.230
come back to the nest with more food.

00:18:58.230 --> 00:19:00.000
They never stop.

00:19:02.880 --> 00:19:05.150
That's bad

00:19:11.830 --> 00:19:13.830
Anybody missing a buffalo bird chick

00:19:21.370 --> 00:19:30.000
Hop in you built a nest on my branches you link

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:31.650
eggs right now?

00:19:31.650 --> 00:19:34.960
What?

00:19:34.960 --> 00:19:42.960
What are you so curious about?

00:19:42.960 --> 00:19:44.640
Martin!

00:19:44.640 --> 00:19:46.200
Martin!

00:19:46.200 --> 00:19:47.090
We're in position.

00:19:47.090 --> 00:19:48.030
Just give the word.

00:19:48.030 --> 00:19:49.080
Okay.

00:19:49.080 --> 00:19:49.910
Okay.

00:19:49.910 --> 00:19:51.910
Ready, and...

00:19:51.910 --> 00:19:59.220
Line.

00:20:00.570 --> 00:20:06.490
Okay, brothers, sisters, it was fun nesting with ya, but I gotta get Moo out of here and back to his parents.

00:20:07.400 --> 00:20:08.670
Till nest time!

00:20:08.670 --> 00:20:10.430
I mean, next time.

00:20:10.430 --> 00:20:11.360
No!

00:20:15.260 --> 00:20:16.640
Got 'em!

00:20:16.640 --> 00:20:17.570
Let's go!

00:20:17.570 --> 00:20:25.960
... to the rescue, guys!

00:20:25.960 --> 00:20:28.870
Spike and Mama Shrike weren't even disturbed at all.

00:20:33.450 --> 00:20:36.340
You laid three eggs on my branches.

00:20:36.340 --> 00:20:39.540
Good idea.

00:20:39.540 --> 00:20:40.340
Take a little break.

00:20:40.340 --> 00:20:41.590
I'll watch the nest.

00:20:41.590 --> 00:20:44.690
After all, like most birds, you'll be sitting on that nest

00:20:44.690 --> 00:20:48.560
almost nonstop for the next two weeks.

00:20:48.560 --> 00:20:51.130
Hey, what are you doing?

00:20:51.130 --> 00:20:52.490
That's not your nest.

00:20:52.490 --> 00:20:53.740
It's hers.

00:20:53.740 --> 00:20:55.280
Wait a second.

00:20:55.280 --> 00:20:57.390
Are you-- you are.

00:20:57.390 --> 00:21:00.000
You laid an egg in her nest, and it looks exactly

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:02.840
like the one Moo hatched out of.

00:21:02.840 --> 00:21:05.510
Ooh, where are you going?

00:21:05.510 --> 00:21:06.570
I can't believe it.

00:21:06.570 --> 00:21:08.290
She did that on purpose.

00:21:08.290 --> 00:21:10.620
Huh?

00:21:10.620 --> 00:21:13.680
That's the strategy of those sneaky buffalo birds.

00:21:13.680 --> 00:21:16.580
They secretly lay their eggs in other birds' nests

00:21:16.580 --> 00:21:20.020
so the other bird ends up raising the chicks for them.

00:21:20.020 --> 00:21:21.860
They're nest parasites.

00:21:21.860 --> 00:21:23.640
Uh-oh.

00:21:23.640 --> 00:21:26.820
Thornbush to Hover Crew, stop the rescue mission.

00:21:26.820 --> 00:21:27.660
- What?

00:21:27.660 --> 00:21:28.430
It's too late.

00:21:28.430 --> 00:21:30.000
We left the nest, see?

00:21:30.480 --> 00:21:33.780
Oh boy, we made a big mistake.

00:21:34.240 --> 00:21:36.780
Moo was supposed to be in that nest the whole time.

00:21:37.130 --> 00:21:37.910
Well, kinda.

00:21:38.290 --> 00:21:40.160
What do you mean? He's not a shrike.

00:21:40.680 --> 00:21:42.740
I know, but it's natural nature.

00:21:43.240 --> 00:21:45.060
Buffalo birds are nest parasites.

00:21:45.430 --> 00:21:47.380
They lay their eggs in other birds' nests.

00:21:47.540 --> 00:21:48.520
That's just what they do.

00:21:49.330 --> 00:21:51.710
Nest parasites. Of course.

00:21:52.050 --> 00:21:52.990
We should have known.

00:21:53.470 --> 00:21:55.570
So Moo didn't need rescuing at all.

00:21:56.220 --> 00:21:56.680
Oops.

00:21:56.800 --> 00:22:00.000
And now, we've got to fix this mistake.

00:22:00.670 --> 00:22:04.660
But how? What are we going to do with a buffalo bird chick?

00:22:04.660 --> 00:22:10.040
We can't put him back now. Then there's only one thing left to do.

00:22:10.040 --> 00:22:19.170
I think we may have just become Moo's new parents.

00:22:19.170 --> 00:22:24.780
Well, that's got to be a record for the most creature mysteries solved in one day.

00:22:24.780 --> 00:22:30.000
Yeah, we found out that Shrikes are the mystery predators that thorn their prey. And we figured

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:35.180
out that buffalo birds, a.k.a. cowbirds, are nest parasites.

00:22:35.180 --> 00:22:40.060
And it says here that they'll do it to over 220 different kinds of birds.

00:22:40.060 --> 00:22:43.090
Man, those buffalo birds are sneaky!

00:22:43.090 --> 00:22:45.590
Uh, can I get a snack over here?

00:22:45.590 --> 00:22:51.370
Coming up, fresh buffalo berry pie a la Shrike.

00:22:51.370 --> 00:22:52.370
Nice!

00:22:52.370 --> 00:22:54.530
I'll leave it here if you want more later.

00:22:54.530 --> 00:22:56.820
Everything okay in bush, Phil?

00:22:56.820 --> 00:22:57.950
I guess so.

00:22:57.950 --> 00:23:00.000
The robin's nest is looking pretty good.

00:23:00.250 --> 00:23:03.840
And Moo is all set up right up there in the new nest we made him.

00:23:03.840 --> 00:23:06.180
Coming!

00:23:06.180 --> 00:23:08.740
I'll tell you something, though.

00:23:08.740 --> 00:23:11.550
It's not easy keeping even one chick fed.

00:23:11.550 --> 00:23:14.480
Is that why buffalo birds do what they do?

00:23:14.480 --> 00:23:16.310
Well, not exactly.

00:23:16.310 --> 00:23:20.330
They do it because buffalo birds are always on the move with the bison herd.

00:23:20.330 --> 00:23:25.030
So they don't stay in one place long enough to build a nest and raise chicks.

00:23:25.030 --> 00:23:28.160
Okay, okay, I'm going!

00:23:28.160 --> 00:23:30.000
Ah, this guy never stops eating.

00:23:30.030 --> 00:23:31.080
Ow.

00:23:31.080 --> 00:23:33.440
And I'm getting a crick in my neck.

00:23:33.440 --> 00:23:36.720
How much longer until these chicks are raised?

00:23:36.720 --> 00:23:40.150
Uh, just a few weeks.

00:23:40.150 --> 00:23:41.990
A few weeks?

00:23:41.990 --> 00:23:48.360
Oh, my aching neck.

00:23:48.360 --> 00:23:53.320
So buffalo birds and loggerhead strikes are two prairie birds with some pretty strange

00:23:53.320 --> 00:23:54.330
ways of doing things.

00:23:54.330 --> 00:24:00.000
The buffalo bird, with her nest parasite strategy, where she lays her eggs secretly in a

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:01.240
another bird's nest.

00:24:01.240 --> 00:24:04.600
And the loggerhead shrike, a songbird with the attitude

00:24:04.600 --> 00:24:08.700
of a raptor who uses thorn bushes in a strange way.

00:24:08.700 --> 00:24:11.110
Let's look for a loggerhead shrike.

00:24:11.110 --> 00:24:14.270
And the best way to do that is to look for their telltale

00:24:14.270 --> 00:24:15.110
signs.

00:24:15.110 --> 00:24:19.990
And that is a small prey animal stuck onto a thorn,

00:24:19.990 --> 00:24:23.210
a piece of barbed wire, or a stick like this.

00:24:23.210 --> 00:24:25.380
Ow.

00:24:25.380 --> 00:24:26.940
Whoa, check it out.

00:24:26.940 --> 00:24:30.000
Down in the ravine, a shrike nest.

00:24:31.310 --> 00:24:33.560
The nest of a loggerhead shrike?

00:24:33.560 --> 00:24:35.490
Do you see any eggs in this nest?

00:24:35.490 --> 00:24:36.470
No, huh?

00:24:36.470 --> 00:24:37.490
They're there.

00:24:37.490 --> 00:24:39.550
Hidden under those fluffy feathers

00:24:39.550 --> 00:24:42.370
could be up to six shrike eggs.

00:24:42.370 --> 00:24:43.810
Here she comes.

00:24:43.810 --> 00:24:45.330
The mother shrike is back.

00:24:45.330 --> 00:24:47.830
It's the mother shrike that sits on the eggs

00:24:47.830 --> 00:24:49.970
and incubates them while the male

00:24:49.970 --> 00:24:52.770
comes back and forth feeding the female.

00:24:52.770 --> 00:24:55.070
It's amazing that shrikes can catch prey

00:24:55.070 --> 00:24:56.840
that's as big as they are.

00:24:56.840 --> 00:25:00.000
Mice, lizards, other birds.

00:25:00.090 --> 00:25:02.840
- That little bird is a big predator.

00:25:02.840 --> 00:25:04.940
- She hardly ever leaves her nest,

00:25:04.940 --> 00:25:07.220
only every now and then to stretch her wings

00:25:07.220 --> 00:25:08.720
and fly off for a bit.

00:25:08.720 --> 00:25:12.280
And it's then when a buffalo bird could strike.

00:25:12.280 --> 00:25:15.840
And the reason buffalo birds are nest parasites is simple.

00:25:15.840 --> 00:25:17.480
They evolved with the bison.

00:25:17.480 --> 00:25:18.980
The bison are roamers,

00:25:18.980 --> 00:25:21.630
so the buffalo birds have to keep up.

00:25:21.630 --> 00:25:24.890
They can't stop and have a nest for three weeks

00:25:24.890 --> 00:25:26.330
and raise their chicks.

00:25:26.330 --> 00:25:28.790
They have to keep up with those bison.

00:25:28.790 --> 00:25:30.000
So they evolved

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:32.650
strategy, nest parasitism.

00:25:32.650 --> 00:25:36.140
Let's just hope they don't find this nest.

00:25:36.140 --> 00:25:37.930
She's back.

00:25:37.930 --> 00:25:40.380
See you later, Shrikes.

00:25:40.380 --> 00:25:42.820
Keep a lookout for those buffalo birds.

00:25:42.820 --> 00:25:44.880
Good luck raising those chicks.

00:25:44.880 --> 00:25:48.320
And remember, even though they may sometimes look ordinary,

00:25:48.320 --> 00:25:50.960
there are creatures that do strange and interesting things

00:25:50.960 --> 00:25:52.600
all around you.

00:25:52.600 --> 00:25:55.180
Keep our creature adventuring.

00:25:55.180 --> 00:25:57.330
We'll see you on the creature trail.

00:26:17.310 --> 00:26:22.780
more about cool animals and collect your own Wild Kratz creature powers, go to

00:26:22.780 --> 00:26:28.800
the Wild Kratz website at pbskids.org. We'll see you there!
